---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('privy')!;
const b = appBySlug('attentive')!;
const path = '/compare/privy-vs-attentive';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/privy-vs-attentive');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/privy-vs-attentive');
const faqs = [
  { question: 'Privy or Attentive for cart recovery?', answer: 'Evaluate Privy for a program connecting storefront capture, email and SMS in one operating workflow. Evaluate Attentive when coordinated messaging across your selected channels is the main requirement. Both now market email and SMS; store size alone does not decide the fit.' },
  { question: 'What should a signup test establish?', answer: 'Trace the exact signup offer into the subscriber record, welcome message and abandoned-checkout journey. Check email and SMS eligibility separately and verify that competing forms do not promise conflicting incentives.' },
  { question: 'How should email and SMS work together?', answer: 'Give each message a purpose and choose one owner for each channel. Select waits from measured event arrival, customer context and applicable sending constraints. Inspect pending actions when a purchase or preference change arrives.' },
  { question: 'How should recovery be measured?', answer: 'Reconcile order IDs, refunds, discounts and overlapping attribution windows. Attributed revenue describes credited orders; a properly designed comparison with a comparable unmessaged group is needed to estimate incremental effects.' },
  { question: 'What matters during migration?', answer: 'Inventory forms, consent records, suppression lists, sender identities and pending messages. Rehearse the handoff with controlled profiles, then disable the outgoing journey before activating its replacement. Keep a rollback owner.' },
  { question: 'How does discount math change the decision?', answer: 'In a hypothetical $435 basket with $130.50 unchanged product cost, full-price product gross profit is $304.50. A 20% discount removes $87 of revenue and leaves $217.50 of product gross profit, before other expenses.' },
  { question: 'Can Privy capture stay while another platform sends?', answer: 'Only adopt that design after verifying the supported transfer path, consent fields, suppression propagation and queue ownership. This comparison does not establish that a particular Privy-to-Attentive connector supports your required behavior.' },
  { question: 'What should the demonstration include?', answer: 'Ask the operator to trace a signup incentive, an email-only subscriber, a dual-channel subscriber who purchases while waiting, a withdrawal of permission and a replayed checkout event. These are proposed acceptance cases, not results from a test we performed.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/compare" class="link text-sm font-bold">← Comparisons</a>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">Decide who owns the journey from signup offer to cart recovery. Compare capture, channel coordination and the work required to move an existing subscriber program.</p>
      <div class="mt-10">
        <TldrBox picks={[{ label: 'Capture-led evaluation', tool: a.name, reason: 'Demonstrate forms, offer delivery and follow-up as one maintainable workflow.' }, { label: 'Channel coordination evaluation', tool: b.name, reason: 'Demonstrate how your selected messaging channels share customer state and stop rules.' }]} />
      </div>
      <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>{a.name}</th><th>{b.name}</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Published scope</td><td>Popups, email, SMS and customer journeys</td><td>Personalized messaging across SMS, email, RCS and push</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Useful demonstration</td><td>Target a capture offer and trace it into welcome and recovery</td><td>Trace customer identity and pending messages across the channels you plan to use</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Existing program</td><td>Check whether consolidating forms and sending reduces operating work</td><td>Check which senders, forms and journeys would actually move</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Commercial comparison</td><td>Request a configuration matching your contacts and sends</td><td>Request a configuration matching your subscribers, channels and message volume</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Start with the signup promise</h2>
        <p>Privy's current site presents capture tools alongside email, SMS and visual journeys. Attentive's current site presents coordinated personalized messaging across several channels, including email and SMS. The old shorthand of basic email versus premium SMS misses both products' published scope. Neither channel availability nor a store-size label proves which workflow your operator can maintain.</p>
        <p>For a store whose biggest leak is visitors leaving before they identify themselves, begin the Privy demonstration at the display: targeting, the promise on the form, the recorded permission and delivery of the promised benefit. For a store already running a subscriber program, begin the Attentive demonstration at customer identity and pending actions across the intended channels. These are evaluation priorities, not exclusive capabilities or a performance ranking.</p>
        <h2>Consolidate the journey or retain a split setup?</h2>
        <p>Draw your current signup surfaces, welcome sequence, cart recovery and promotional calendar before replacing anything. A capture tool and a sender can each look correct in isolation while giving the same shopper conflicting offers. Decide which system issues the signup benefit, which records redemption and which owns recovery after the first checkout.</p>
        <p>If retaining Privy forms while evaluating Attentive sending, ask for the supported data path and inspect the actual fields transferred. A subscriber count is insufficient: the receiving system needs the correct channel eligibility, source and suppression state. This page has not verified that a particular connector transfers those fields or cancels queued actions. Treat a split configuration as a proposed design until those behaviors are demonstrated.</p>
        <p>If consolidating, include the cost of rebuilding displays and journeys, not just importing contacts. Preserve exclusions and offer promises through the cutover. A contact who previously declined SMS must not become eligible merely because an email address appears in the new system.</p>
        <h2>Proposed acceptance cases for capture and recovery</h2>
        <p>The following cases are a demonstration plan, not merchant observations. Use controlled profiles and inspect records, queues and previews before enabling live messages.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Signup offer followed by abandonment:</strong> submit the live-design form, start a checkout and inspect welcome and recovery branches. Confirm the promised offer is delivered once, that its redemption rules are clear and that a second sender does not introduce a competing code.</li>
          <li><strong>Email-only subscriber:</strong> identify the profile at checkout without adding SMS permission. Inspect channel eligibility and verify that the proposed journey does not create an SMS action from email permission alone.</li>
          <li><strong>Dual-channel subscriber who buys while waiting:</strong> queue recovery, then place the controlled order. Trace the purchase through every active sender and confirm remaining recovery actions stop. Record observed propagation delay rather than asserting a universal cancellation deadline.</li>
          <li><strong>Preference change after signup:</strong> withdraw the relevant permission before a pending action executes. Check the current state at the sender, including any retained capture system, and identify who resolves a failed update.</li>
          <li><strong>Repeated checkout event:</strong> replay the controlled event or reconnect the integration. Inspect for duplicate journeys and stale cart contents. Document the event identity and re-entry rule so a retry does not silently become another incentive opportunity.</li>
        </ol>
        <h2>Choose a schedule from the actual journey</h2>
        <p>Measure when the identifiable checkout event reaches the sender before adding waits. Keep an integration delay separate from a wait inside the journey. Review existing welcome and campaign messages before assigning recovery touches; the customer experiences all of them, even if different tools report them separately.</p>
        <p>Email can carry product details, shipping explanations or an answer about an unredeemed signup offer. An SMS branch needs a distinct purpose and current eligibility, with applicable quiet-hour and sending controls configured for the intended market. Do not create urgency unless an actual deadline exists. Choose timing and frequency from the store's buying cycle and measured response, rather than inheriting a fixed ladder from a comparison page.</p>
        <p>Inspect changed inventory and checkout links as well as purchase status. If the cart can no longer be fulfilled, the recovery message should not promise the original order is ready. Keep stock notifications separate from recovery so a shopper's interest in an unavailable product does not become an unrelated discount sequence.</p>
        <h2>Model the signup benefit before escalating the offer</h2>
        <p>Hypothetical example with unchanged product cost: a $435 basket at 70% product gross margin has $130.50 of product cost and $304.50 of full-price product gross profit. A 20% discount removes $87 of revenue. Discounted revenue is $348 and product gross profit is $217.50. The loss is $87, not 20% of the original profit.</p>
        <p>This matters when the signup promise already contains an incentive. Check whether a recovery code replaces it, stacks with it or fails against the same products. Model the actual redemption rules rather than treating welcome and cart offers as independent budgets. Shipping subsidies, payment fees, returns and messaging costs are excluded from the example and belong in the store's final contribution calculation.</p>
        <h2>Compare credited orders and operating cost separately</h2>
        <p>Reconcile order IDs across both platforms and Shopify before adding reported recovery revenue. Document attribution windows, whether the order used a code, refunds and messages from other campaigns. A credited order does not establish that the shopper needed the message to return. If volume permits a controlled incrementality study, define the eligible population, assignment method and outcome window before launch; there is no universal holdout percentage supplied by this comparison.</p>
        <p>Request matched quotes using the same contacts, subscriber eligibility, countries, email volume and SMS assumptions. Include recurring platform charges, usage, required support, migration work and any retained sender. Review a quiet month and a promotion month. Neither an old entry price nor a public rating tells you whether this operating model is affordable.</p>
        <h2>Migration acceptance and the final decision</h2>
        <p>Inventory active forms, sender identities, subscriber records, suppression lists, promised benefits and pending messages. Rehearse the cutover with the cases above. Name the person who disables outgoing journeys and the person who checks replacement queues. Keep the outgoing system's suppressions available for reconciliation and document a rollback that cannot restart duplicate recovery.</p>
        <p>Choose Privy if its demonstrated capture-to-message workflow fits the operator and removes the handoffs you actually have. Choose Attentive if its demonstrated coordination across your selected channels fits the program and the migration cost is justified. If neither resolves a required lifecycle workflow, evaluate that missing requirement explicitly before adding a third platform such as Sequenzy; adding another layer creates more ownership and synchronization work and is not an automatic improvement.</p>
        <h2>Product scope sources</h2>
        <p>Reviewed 24 November 2026: <a href="https://www.privy.com/">Privy's published product overview</a> and <a href="https://www.attentive.com/">Attentive's published product overview</a>. These support the broad product scope described here. They do not establish your account's connector behavior, commercial terms, permission configuration or recovery performance; those remain acceptance work for the proposed setup.</p>
      </article>
      <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Privy vs Attentive FAQ" />
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
